<section image-layout="blank" id="DePWSpd22N" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="gQZ7gOjImT"><h2>Solve the case—then keep one question open</h2><p font-size="lg">Work in pairs. Use one clue from the map, objects, timeline, or viewpoints for each answer.</p><grid marker="number" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" spacing="medium" padding-top="34pt"><grid-item id="LBC_Z0oFrn" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Find the place</h4><p font-size="18pt">Which Brazilian state is at the center of this story?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="e2ZRfMfON5" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Find the causes</h4><p font-size="18pt">Name two kinds of disagreement behind the conflict.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="dnLxQ_9zgh" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Compare viewpoints</h4><p font-size="18pt">Why might the same event feel different to a free seller and an enslaved worker?</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="NaetB0pnn8" background-color="accent" padding="16pt 20pt"><p role="eyebrow">The case is not completely closed</p><h3>A date can end a conflict. It cannot tell every story.</h3><p font-size="18pt"><b>Final detective question:</b> Whose voice might still be missing?</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Quiz check: Rio Grande do Sul. Possible causes include trade and tax disputes, regional power, and disagreement over separation from Brazil. Viewpoints differed because people did not have equal rights or choices. Invite children to suggest other missing civilian voices without inventing biographies.</p></notes></section>